<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/deviance-and-crime">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Crime is where sociology's questions sharpen: <strong>what counts as deviance is a social
    decision, enforcement is unevenly distributed, and the response can become part of the
    problem</strong>. Three questions organise the unit — what are crime, deviance and social
    control; what patterns and explanations does the evidence show; and how do different
    societies deal with crime — and the modern field's signature is that the last one is
    answered experimentally: interventions are trialled, measured, replicated or discarded.
    It is sociology with an evidence base sharp enough to embarrass its own traditions.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="definitions, labelling, mass incarceration, evidence-based prevention" widget="equationStep" config={crimeCfg} />

  <h2>Crime, deviance and social control</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Deviance</strong> is violation of social norms; <strong>crime</strong> is
    violation of codified law. The first is everywhere and constant; the second is a
    moving legal boundary — prohibition criminalised a beverage and then un-criminalised
    it; homosexuality was a crime across the West within living memory; cannabis is being
    re-legalised jurisdiction by jurisdiction now, with no biological change in the plant.
    Definitions therefore track power and morality campaigns as much as harm (though
    severity does correlate with harm — homicide is criminal everywhere). Durkheim's
    founding move was to treat deviance as <em>normal and functional</em>: every society
    produces it, and society <em>uses</em> it — the public trial, the headline, the
    shared outrage are rituals that redraw the collective boundary and reaffirm what the
    group values; a society of "saints" would define trivial deviations as crimes.
    Punishment, in this reading, is a solidarity ceremony — which explains its theatrical
    character and why it often seems designed for audiences rather than deterrence.
    <strong>Social control</strong> is the enforcement side of the same coin: informal
    control (gossip, shame, exclusion — the everyday policing this unit's identity
    lessons described) does most of the work; formal control (police, courts, prisons)
    is the state's professional layer. When informal norms are perceived as threatened,
    Cohen's <strong>moral panic</strong> (1972) names the escalation: a folk devil
    (mods and rockers, then muggers, then various drug users), exaggerated media
    amplification, disproportionate response — which frequently manufactures the
    very deviance it fears.
  </p>

  <h2>Patterns: who is deviant, statistically</h2>
  <p>
    The regularities are among the most stable in social science. The
    <strong>age-crime curve</strong>: offending rises through adolescence, peaks in the
    late teens, and declines steeply through the twenties — in every country and every
    decade measured; a small high-rate group (roughly 5-6% of males, per the Cambridge
    Study in Delinquent Development) accounts for a large share of offences.
    The <strong>gender gap</strong>: males commit the large majority of recorded crime
    everywhere, a regularity no single theory has fully nailed (opportunity, socialisation
    into risk, and testosteronal-average differences all contribute, in contested
    proportions). The <strong>class skew of enforcement</strong>: recorded crime is
    concentrated in deprived areas — but so is <em>policing</em>, which is part of the
    measurement. Sutherland's founding correction (1939) still stands:
    <strong>white-collar crime</strong> — financial fraud, antitrust violations, safety
    and environmental offences committed by the respectable — dwarfs street crime in
    monetary terms and, through occupational deaths and unsafe products, plausibly in
    lives, yet is investigated, prosecuted and punished at a fraction of the rate. The
    pattern is not that the poor are more criminal; it is that the definitions and
    enforcement priorities are drawn where power is not. Sampson's
    <strong>collective efficacy</strong> research adds the sharpest neighbourhood
    finding: crime concentrates where mutual trust and willingness to intervene have
    collapsed — and concentrated disadvantage erodes exactly that trust, linking
    inequality to crime through a mechanism you can point at.
  </p>

  <h2>The Chicago school: crime has an address</h2>
  <p>
    The first great spatial discovery came from mapping. Park and Burgess's Chicago
    (1920s) modelled the city as concentric zones; Shaw and McKay then charted
    juvenile delinquency by neighbourhood and found the striking thing:
    <strong>delinquency rates were stable by place, not by population</strong> —
    neighbourhoods kept high rates across decades of complete ethnic turnover, which
    ruled out the offenders' origins as the cause and indicted the neighbourhood
    itself. <strong>Social disorganisation</strong> was the mechanism: poverty,
    residential churn and mixed land use erode the local networks that supervise
    teenagers, maintain norms and intervene in trouble — crime as a property of
    weak collective infrastructure, not of the people passing through it. The
    theory was long neglected, then spectacularly revived in Sampson's
    <strong>collective efficacy</strong> work (noted below in the explanations
    section) — a Chicago re-survey finding trust-and-intervene neighbourhoods
    lower in violence at equal poverty, and policy descendants in everything
    from community policing to place-based investment. Its deeper gift is
    methodological: the demonstration that ecological data — properly controlled —
    can distinguish place effects from people effects, the analysis this unit's
    other lessons keep needing.
  </p>

  <h2>Explanations: the four families</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Strain theories</strong> (Merton, 1938): society sells one success goal
    (material success) while rationing the legitimate means; blocked individuals adapt —
    and "innovation" (illegal means to legitimate goals) predicts precisely the crime
    class that enforcement records. <strong>Subcultural theories</strong> (Cohen,
    Cloward & Ohlin): blocked youth form groups whose status systems invert the
    mainstream's — delinquency as a solution to status frustration, learned like any
    culture. <strong>Control theories</strong> (Hirschi, 1969) invert the question:
    since deviance is easy, the puzzle is conformity — people refrain when
    <em>bonds</em> hold: attachment to others, commitment to futures, involvement, and
    belief in the rules. Its strongest modern support is Sampson & Laub's finding that
    marriage and steady work turn criminal careers off — bonds, literally. 
    <strong>Labelling theory</strong> (Becker, 1963) shifts attention from the act to
    the reaction: <em>primary</em> deviance (situational, common) becomes
    <em>secondary</em> deviance (identity-organised) when getting caught recasts the
    person; the label closes legitimate doors, and closed doors push. The theories are
    rivals on paper and complementary in evidence: strain and subculture explain where
    offending starts, control explains what stops it, labelling explains what the
    system does to it afterwards — a division of labour you will see again in the
    policy section.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> crime rose because society got worse. Measured crime
  fell steeply across the rich world from the early 1990s to the late 2010s (US
  violent crime roughly halved) during decades of visible disorder and anxiety —
  while imprisonment <em>rose</em>. Perception and reality diverge systematically,
  and media amplification is a first-order variable, not background noise.</p>

  <h2>Mass incarceration: the natural experiment</h2>
  <p>
    The United States ran the field's largest uncontrolled experiment. Its imprisonment
    rate — around 670-700 per 100,000 including jails, roughly 0.7% of the population —
    was for decades the world's highest (El Salvador's recent mass detentions have since
    rivalled it): the US holds about 5% of humanity and, at peak, roughly 20% of its
    prisoners. The racial disparity is part of the number: Black Americans are imprisoned
    at roughly five times the white rate. Crucially, the growth was not driven by crime —
    crime <em>fell</em> through most of the prison surge — but by policy: mandatory
    minimums, three-strikes laws, truth-in-sentencing, and the drug war multiplying
    admissions for offences with short exposure windows. The National Academies'
    consensus review (2014) concluded the four-decade growth was overwhelmingly
    sentencing-driven, and that marginal imprisonment reduces crime — at sharply
    <em>diminishing</em> returns, with collateral costs (family disruption, employment
    scars, community-level criminogenic effects — clearances for the labelling loop)
    that the deterrence arithmetic does not count. The prosecutor, meanwhile, holds the
    machine: some 90-95% of convictions arrive by plea bargain, so charge decisions —
    not trials, not judges — set most outcomes, a decisive sociological fact invisible
    at street level.
  </p>

  <h2>How societies deal with crime — and what the trials show</h2>
  <p>
    Systems cluster by philosophy. The <strong>punitive</strong> model (US, England and
    Wales to a lesser degree) emphasises incapacitation and desert; the
    <strong>welfare-rehabilitative</strong> model (Scandinavia) treats imprisonment as
    primarily a re-entry problem — Norwegian prisons normalise life (keys, jobs,
    education), and two-year reoffending runs around 20%, against US rearrest studies
    finding about two-thirds of released prisoners rearrested within three years. Careful
    comparison (different measures, populations, welfare states) forbids attributing the
    whole gap to prison style — but the pattern survives every adjustment so far.
    Japan's low-crime equilibrium runs on intense informal control plus a
    prosecutor-dominated, probation-heavy system with its own controversies (near-total
    conviction rates, hostage-justice criticism). <strong>Restorative
    justice</strong> — victim-offender conferencing — is the tested alternative:
    randomised trials (Sherman & Strang's programme of RCTs, synthesised in the Campbell
    review) find modest but consistent reoffending reductions for property and violent
    crime, much higher victim satisfaction, and cost-effectiveness — one of the field's
    genuinely replicated successes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The evidence hierarchy for prevention, stated plainly. <strong>Works:</strong>
    focused deterrence (Boston's Operation Ceasefire: youth homicides fell by roughly
    two-thirds by making consequences swift, certain and credible for a small targeted
    group while offering real services); cognitive-behavioural programmes with
    high-risk individuals (consistent meta-analytic effects on reoffending); hot-spots
    policing (small, replicated crime reductions); therapeutic rather than punitive
    juvenile responses; and — the surprise that teaches the most — <strong>lead
    abatement</strong>: phasing leaded petrol out, preceded two decades later by crime
    declines across many countries, and econometric work (Reyes, Nevin) attributes a
    substantial share of the 1990s crime drop to childhood lead reduction — a toxin
    harming impulse control was, in effect, crime policy. Summer youth employment
    programmes cut violent-crime arrests by about a third in Chicago's randomised
    trial. <strong>Does not work or backfires:</strong> Scared Straight — randomised
    trials and the Petrosino meta-analysis found it <em>increased</em> offending
    (intimidation hardened attitudes); blanket severity (per the deterrence evidence,
    certainty beats severity); and pure surveillance without legitimacy (procedural
    justice research — Tyler's — shows perceived fairness drives compliance better
    than fear does). The unit's through-line: responses are also causes, and can be
    audited like anything else.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The crime-drop whodunit.</strong> No consensus explains the great 1990s
    decline; the candidate list is itself a map of the field — increased imprisonment
    and policing (real but partial), the lead hypothesis (strong timing evidence),
    demographic change, economic improvement (weaker than expected), the legalised
    abortion hypothesis (Donohue & Levitt — influential and contested), and the
    stabilising of crack-era dynamics. Plausibly several causes at once; the episode's
    lesson is epistemological: even a half-billion-person natural experiment resists a
    single-variable verdict.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Technology's feedback loops.</strong> Predictive policing trained on past
    arrests patrols past arrests' neighbourhoods and finds more of what it patrols —
    labelling theory with an API. Body cameras, risk-assessment scores (COMPAS and the
    ProPublica fairness controversy) and pretrial algorithms inherit the same problem:
    they measure the system's past behaviour, not crime itself. The emerging
    methodology — auditing algorithms the way audit studies audit landlords — is
    sociology adapting to software.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can the certainty-severity insight be exported to
    white-collar crime, where detection is rare by design? Will falling formal crime
    keep diverging from online harms (fraud is now a majority-impact crime class,
    barely policed)? And can legitimacy be built deliberately — the procedural-justice
    evidence suggests yes, but it is slow work in systems asked to be fast. Durkheim
    would recognise the frame: every one of these questions is still about where
    societies draw the line, and what drawing it publicly does to the people on each
    side of it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Durkheim, The Rules of Sociological Method (1895); Merton (1938); Cohen, Folk Devils and Moral Panics (1972); Becker, Outsiders (1963); Hirschi (1969); Sampson & Laub (1993); Sutherland (1949); National Academies, The Growth of Incarceration in the United States (2014); Nagin (2013) on deterrence; Kennedy, Deterrence and Crime Prevention; Sherman & Strang, restorative justice RCTs and the Campbell review; Petrosino et al. on Scared Straight; Reyes (2007); Heller et al. (2017); Tyler, Why People Obey the Law.</p>

